speechtext:
When the House rose at six o'clock I was reading a dispatch to the Winnipeg ' Free Press,' dated January 25, which showed that on that date a condition of blockade existed in the terminals at Fort William. How long previous that condition had existed we are not able to say. But I assume, for the purposes of my calculation, that it began not later than January 10. At that time there was about 18 million bushels of all kinds of grain in storage in the terminals, and I find by the latest report of the Department of Trade and Commerce that on the week ending February 2 the amount in s'ore at the terminal elevators was still approximately 18 million bushels, showing that whatever the condition was at the time mentioned, it still continued up to February 5. What I desire to bring to the attention of the House is the statement made by the ' Free Press ' that the railway company was not able to forward the grain from Fort William at the rate of 200 cars a day, and allowing a period from January 10 to April 1 for the relief of the blockade conditions in time to be of any use
in the case of damp grain, we have eleven and a half weeks. Moving that grain at the rate at which this despatch says it can be moved, something less than 200 cars a day, the railway company would be able to imove 12 million /bushels during that time, and they would relieve the terminal storage to that extent. If they were also able then to bring 12 million bushels forward from the wheat fields to the terminals they would relieve the conditions in the wheat fields to that extent. But on the statement of Mr. Thompson, of the Ogilvie Milling Company, that on January 10 there were 30 million bushels still in the wheat fields, that is to say, in the farmer's hands, either in the stook or threshed, there would still be from 13 to 18 million bushels left in the fields or on the farmer's hands on the 1st of April, after which date the grain that was damp and liable to heat would practically be useless for commercial purposes. Unless damp grain is kept in condition by cold weather or reaches a drying plant before the 1st of April, it has, in all human probability, become destroyed, so far as commercial value is concerned. Therefore, on the statement which I have before me, and I am sure it is a very moderate statement, the country is face to face with a probable loss of some 13 to 18 million bushels of wheat by reason of this blockade.
I have said that the loss to the farmer on the expectation of his crop was estimated at about 25 million bushels. At 60 cents a bushel the loss to the farmer will be $15,000,000. But, unfortunately, besides the shortage of the crop is the loss to the farmer by reason of depreciated quality, owing to the unfavourab'e season of late growth and harvest. What the depreciation actually is must be a matter of estimate. But we can form a fair estimate fey taking the quality of grain in store at the terminals at a certain date. I find also in a statement of the Department of Trade and Commerce that theie was in store in the terminals in January, twelve and a half million bushels of wheat. Of this, four and a half million bushels graded No. 3 and upwards;
3	and a quarter millions graded 4, 5 and 6;
4	and three-quarter millions were below No. 6. That is to say, speaking roughly, one-third of the total was good milling wheat, one-third was poor milling wheat, and one-third was unfit for milling. As to the depreciation in price because of the depreciation in quality, I quote Winnipeg prices of January 25. No. 1 Northern was worth 961 cts.; No. 3 Northern, 88 cts.; No. 5, 73 cts.; Feed, 57 cts.; Feed tough, 51 cts; a spread of 8 cents between 1 and 3 Northern, of 15 cents between 3 and 5 Northern, of 16 cents between 5 Northern and Feed, and a spread of 6 cents between

